WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.260
Light hyperpolarizes a rod.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.340
Yet light depolarizes these ganglion cells.

3
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.700
How can the same light push two cells opposite ways?

4
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:13.910
Start with the wiring.

5
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.350
Rods and cones signal to bipolar cells.

6
00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.150
Bipolar cells signal to the ganglion cell's dendrites.

7
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.300
Melanopsin sits in the ganglion cell's own membrane.

8
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.040
So light reaches the cell by two routes.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.620
But each cell has one axon, one exit.

10
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.400
Across the population, those exits reach the clock and the pupil centre.

11
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:39.513
That is the one idea of this lesson.

12
00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:43.913
An ipRGC pools two light signals into one output.

13
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:49.377
One input is the rod and cone relay, usually faster and more sensitive.

14
00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:53.677
The other is melanopsin, usually slower and less sensitive.

15
00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:56.597
Both can be sustained, and both adapt.

16
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.460
In mice, these cells are the main route by which either signal reaches the clock and

17
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.460
the pupil.

18
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:08.683
So removing the pigment, or the rods and cones, leaves the other input working

19
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:11.363
alone. Removing the cell removes both.

20
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:16.663
Now the polarity. In a rod, light closes cation channels.

21
00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:20.583
Less positive charge enters, and the rod hyperpolarizes.

22
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:24.747
In an M1 cell, light opens channels instead.

23
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:27.507
Melanopsin is an opsin.

24
00:01:28.247 --> 00:01:32.507
But it is closer to the visual opsins of insects than to rod and cone opsins.

25
00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:37.437
It activates a G protein, inferred to be of the Gq family.

26
00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.697
That activates phospholipase C beta 4.

27
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:45.727
Then TRPC6 and TRPC7 channels open.

28
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:51.777
Without phospholipase C beta 4, the M1 cell's own light response essentially

29
00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:52.777
disappeared.

30
00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:59.247
A fly photoreceptor works much the same way, with Gq, phospholipase C and TRP

31
00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:00.247
channels.

32
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:06.110
Predict. These channels pass cations, and their current reverses near zero

33
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.590
millivolts. Opening them moves the voltage which way?

34
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.230
Recall the balance-point rule.

35
00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:22.850
When the voltage is steady, with no injected current, it sits at a weighted average

36
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.390
of reversal potentials.

37
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.370
The weights are the conductances.

38
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:28.753
Here that is near zero.

39
00:02:29.393 --> 00:02:32.713
So the voltage moves up, away from the potassium potential.

40
00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:34.713
The cell depolarizes.

41
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:41.817
In Another subtype, M4, may also been reported to close potassium leak channels.

42
00:02:42.337 --> 00:02:47.917
That depolarizes too, by the same rule.

43
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.340
Which other channels also contribute in M4 is still disputed.

44
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.400
Light hyperpolarizes a rod and depolarizes an M1 cell.

45
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.840
And both of the cell's inputs leave by one exit.
